@fig-dashboard-architecture shows that the dashboard is positioned at the end of the forecasting workflow. Its purpose is not to generate predictions, but rather to facilitate their interpretation and operational use.

=== Visualization Components

The visualization layer relies on Altair@altair and VegaFusion@vegafusion to efficiently handle datasets containing multiple hierarchical levels and long forecasting horizons.

The dashboard is primarily composed of two synchronized graphical elements:

- an observed series chart;
- a forecast chart.

Historical observations are represented using a line chart, while forecasted values are displayed as an additional superimposed layer.

An example of the resulting visualization is shown in @fig-altair-dashboard.

#figure(
image("../../images/altair_dashboard.png"),
caption: [Example of the Altair-based interactive forecasting interface.]
) <fig-altair-dashboard>

@fig-altair-dashboard illustrates the integration of historical observations and reconciled forecasts within a single interactive visualization. The layered representation facilitates the comparison between past dynamics and expected future values.

=== User Interaction Layer

Interactivity is implemented through dropdown menus allowing users to filter forecasts according to both hierarchical dimensions and forecasting models.

The available filters include:

- deposit center (*Centre_Agence_depot*);
- destination country (*Destination*);
- forecasting model.

The model selector enables users to visualize forecasts produced by any candidate model included in the evaluation framework, such as statistical, machine learning, or deep learning approaches.

Users can therefore navigate seamlessly between:

- national forecasts;
- agency-level forecasts;
- destination-level forecasts;
- origin-destination combinations;
- alternative forecasting models.

The filtering mechanism operates directly on the reconciled display dataset. Consequently, every visualization remains hierarchically coherent regardless of the selected aggregation level or forecasting method.

